 data-v-9019dc97=3D"" class=3D"markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><d=
iv data-v-048cb31c=3D"" class=3D"markdown"><div class=3D"paragraph" dir=3D"=
auto">I have the core design rules. Key constraints for my mockup widget:</=
div><ul start=3D"1"><li><div class=3D"paragraph" dir=3D"auto">No hardcoded =
colors =E2=80=94 use <code data-v-bfaabce6=3D"" data-v-048cb31c=3D"" class=
=3D"segment-code-inline">var(--kimi-color-*)</code> tokens. Black/white/gra=
y primary; accents only from tokens (avoid heavy gold washes; I can use gol=
d as small accent dots/chips at low tint =E2=80=94 but "accent colors come =
only from runtime tokens (never invented)". Hmm =E2=80=94 her brand gold #E=
8D574 isn't a runtime token. But this widget is a MOCKUP of HER app's desig=
n (gold brand) =E2=80=94 "Unless the user has given very explicit, precise =
styling instructions" =E2=80=94 she hasn't; but the widget's purpose is pre=
viewing her brand design. The rule says never invent colors. I could use <c=
ode data-v-bfaabce6=3D"" data-v-048cb31c=3D"" class=3D"segment-code-inline"=
>--kimi-color-warning</code> (yellowish?) as stand-in for gold... risky to =
guess. Alternative: represent the gold accent via a small legend "gold acce=
nt" using color-mix of warning token, or just use neutral chrome for the mo=
ckup structure and mark accent zones with an outline + label. Cleanest: bui=
ld the layout in black/white/gray with ONE accent element using <code data-=
v-bfaabce6=3D"" data-v-048cb31c=3D"" class=3D"segment-code-inline">var(--ki=
mi-color-warning)</code> tinted, labeled as "your gold lives here" =E2=80=
=94 keeps design-system compliance while communicating placement. Actually =
tokens likely include --kimi-color-warning (yellow-ish). Using it at 15% ti=
nt for active chips approximates gold nicely.</div></li></ul><div class=3D"=
paragraph" dir=3D"auto">Also avoid glassmorphism per the design system... b=
ut her app design itself would use her own brand style (the widget is a moc=
kup of HER product, not a Kimi-native widget). The design system is strict =
though. I'll keep the mockup clean/flat with fine borders =E2=80=94 actuall=
y a flat, clean, modern mockup IS a good direction anyway; and note in pros=
e that in her real code we can do soft blur/gold. The design system says av=
oid heavy blur/glass =E2=80=94 so flat-clean mockup it is; and honestly mod=
ern 2026 camera UIs are going clean minimal anyway.</div><div class=3D"para=
graph" dir=3D"auto">No emoji as UI icons =E2=80=94 use the Kimi icon librar=
y. Let me check the manifest quickly for relevant icons: camera, video, ref=
resh/flip, close/x, lightning/flash, grid. Read icon manifest.</div><div cl=
ass=3D"paragraph" dir=3D"auto">Then build the widget: a phone-frame mockup =
of the redesigned camera modal with variant switcher (2-3 layout options), =
interactive (shutter button press anim, mode toggle Photo/Video sliding). K=
eep compact. The widget shows "what the new camera could look like" =E2=80=
=94 variants: "Clean studio", "Bold minimal", maybe "Classic+ refresh". Let=
 me do 3 variants toggleable. Use sendPrompt for her pick? The contract: se=
ndPrompt pushes user's next intent. I can add buttons "I like this one" tha=
t sendPrompt("I pick variant X =E2=80=94 let's build it"). Nice.</div><div =
class=3D"paragraph" dir=3D"auto">Icons needed: x (close), lightning-bolt, a=
rrows/flip, camera, video, grid, timer, aperture. Let me read manifest.</di=
v></div></div></div></div> <div class=3D"scroll-edge-fade scroll-edge-fade-=
bottom" aria-hidden=3D"true"></div><div class=3D"scroll-edge-fade-measure-s=
entinel" aria-hidden=3D"true"></div></div></div></div><div data-v-afe3a0d2=
